  "query_preview": "..\n\nA cold front will sweep across lower MI today between about 18Z and \n00Z bringing with it a scattered showers and gusty southwesterly \nwinds. The front was meridional passing through MN and WI today with \nstrong fgen and a strong low level jet feeding the front. As the \nsurface low tracks from Lake Superior tonight up to western Quebec by \nthis afternoon, it will drag the front through the area which will \nbecome increasingly more positively tilted. The fgen weakens and \nbecomes disorganized and the low level jet lifts northeastward away \nfrom lower MI. A weak mid level wave will move into the area as the \nfront passes this afternoon which will help daytime heating to bring \nvery weak instability over the area with MUCAPE up to a couple \nhundred J/kg. Lapse rates are still in the 6-7 C/km range for the \nmost part in the narrow moist layer ahead of the front. Expectations \nare for scattered showers to move into Saginaw Valley this morning \nbefore spreading south and east across the area with the front \nthrough the afternoon. A few embedded non-severe thunderstorms may \naccompany the showers, especially later this afternoon around the \nDetroit area as the front tries to re-intensify before exiting to \nthe southeast. \n\nHigh temperatures will rise into the 70s once more today with the \nlater frontal passage. The next ridge building in behind the front \nout of Canada will bring a cooler airmass with 850mb temps down to \naround 5C. Lows tonight will respond dropping into the mid to upper \n30s for most locations away from the Detroit heat island. Highs \nFriday will then struggle to hit 60s, which is actually still above \nnormal for late October. Mostly clear skies to start Friday night \nwill allow for a quick drop in temps once again down to around \nfreezing in the Thumb. Clouds will start filling back in across the \nsouth so will limit the cooling potential closer to the Ohio border \nso lows may struggle to drop below 40. \n\nThe aforementioned clouds are associated with a southern stream \nfront lifting into the Ohio Valley. Elevated portions will extend \ntoward southern MI but strong surface high and overall upper level \nheight rises will result in strong stability and prevent precip from \nmaking it into southeast MI. This ridging will hold into the new \nweek keeping temperatures running above normal through this \ntime. The next system slated to enter the region Tuesday.",
